.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KUBE-JOIN 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kube-join
.Nd join this machine to a kldload Kubernetes cluster, as a worker or another control plane
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -control-plane
.Ar endpoint
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
joins this machine to the cluster whose control plane answers at
.Ar endpoint
\(em the cluster's control-plane address, set by
.Xr kube-init 8 ,
so that the join does not depend on one node; a node's own address
works too.
In order it:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
checks that kubeadm and containerd are here and the machine is not
already in a cluster;
.It
snapshots every dataset of the root's pool, as
.Ar pool Ns Pa @pre-kube-join- Ns Ar timestamp ,
and warns if it cannot;
.It
fetches the join command from the control plane over ssh as root: the
one
.Xr kube-init 8
saved, or a fresh one;
.It
checks that it is exactly kubeadm's
.Ql kubeadm join host:port --token token --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:hash ,
and refuses anything else;
.It
runs
.Ql kubeadm join
with those values.
.El
.Pp
With
.Fl -control-plane
it first checks that the cluster was made with a control-plane address,
without which kubeadm cannot add a control plane; fetches a new
certificate key, since kubeadm's lasts about two hours; joins as a
control plane advertising this machine's address toward
.Ar endpoint ;
copies the admin configuration to root and the first ordinary user; and
lets workloads run on the new control plane, as
.Xr kube-init 8
does for the first.
.Pp
It runs itself under
.Xr sudo 8 .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -control-plane , Fl -cp
Join as another control plane.
etcd wants an odd number of them: go from one to three.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit 0, without root.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when joined; 1 on a failure or refusal, named, and on a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
In a Fedora 44 container, with
.Xr ssh 1
replaced by a script that answers as a control plane would, but with
.Ql ; touch /pwned
on the end, and kubeadm by one that records its arguments:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kube-join 10.0.0.1
[kube-join] Fetching join command from 10.0.0.1...
[kube-join] the join command from 10.0.0.1 is not kubeadm's -- refusing to run it: kubeadm join 10.0.0.1:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Pp
Nothing was run: no
.Pa /pwned ,
and kubeadm was never called.
With the
.Ql ; touch /pwned
taken off, kubeadm was called with exactly
.Ql join 10.0.0.1:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:bbb\&...
and it exited 0.
.Pp
Joining for real; not run for this page:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kube-join 192.168.122.240
# kube-join --control-plane 192.168.122.240
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kubeadm 1 ,
.Xr kube-cluster 8 ,
.Xr kube-init 8 ,
.Xr kube-network 8 ,
.Xr kube-reset 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: the join command fetched from the
control plane was run with
.Ql eval
as root, so whatever answered at
.Ar endpoint
could run any command on the joining machine \(em shown in a container,
where the installed version ran the
.Ql touch
and reported a successful join.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
The ssh to the control plane does not check its host key, and falls back
to a fixed root password: a machine that answers at
.Ar endpoint
in its place can still hand over a join command for a cluster of its
own, and this machine would join it.
.Xr kube-cluster 8 ,
which builds clusters from goldens, joins its workers itself and does not
use
.Nm .
